<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastphalia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ostfalen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eastphalia (Ostfalen) was the eastern division of Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Angria</a> it made up the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. The name survives as a historical and dialect region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Eastphalia covered the eastern shires (Gaue) of the stem duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, roughly between the River Leine in the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> in the east, in what is now south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Its name probably means east plain: falen is a Germanic word for flat or level ground. The North German Plain of Eastphalia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> contrasts with the hilly Central Uplands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and Thuringia to the south. Its shires included Astfala around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, the Harzgau around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>, the Salzgau and the Hassegau around Mansfeld.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the Royal Frankish Annals and the Saxon Poet, the Eastphalians under Hessi gave hostages and swore loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> at the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oker">Oker</a> in 775. Hessi later became a Christian and was made a count, and the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> were founded east of the Oker in 804 and 815. The lands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> were the hereditary lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a>, the first Saxon duke to become King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 919, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a> that followed him.</p><p>Bordering the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a> along and beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, Eastphalia became the starting point of the German Ostsiedlung. Sometime after 965 Otto I entrusted the defence of its north-eastern parts, later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dietrich-of-haldensleben">Dietrich of Haldensleben</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> was placed under the imperial ban in 1180, the region broke up into smaller states, among them the Duchy of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, the counties of Anhalt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wernigerode">Wernigerode</a> and Blankenburg, the Imperial city of Goslar, the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and the prince-bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halberstadt">Halberstadt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, whose name lived on in the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>'s Duchy of Westphalia, the name Eastphalia fell out of use after 1180. Linguists revived it in the 19th century for the Eastphalian dialect of West Low German. The Ottonian abbeys and castles of the region include three World Heritage Sites: the medieval town of Goslar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a>, and St. Mary's Cathedral with St. Michael's Church at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eastphalia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its main regions were Westphalia, Engern (the land of the Angrarii on the middle Weser), <b>Eastphalia</b> and Nordalbingia, north of the Elbe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They were later absorbed into the Saxons; their name survives in Engern (Angria), one of the three provinces of Old Saxony, alongside Westphalia and <b>Eastphalia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/angrivarii">Angrivarii</a></li></ul></section>
